raptormark: pick one alias per address deterministically.

ELFObject::LoadELF sorts func_symbols by address alone. The vector holds every
BSF_FUNCTION symbol LoadStaticSymbolsBFD found plus everything the eh_frame
recovery contributed, so an address named by several symbols appears several
times -- glibc aliases heavily (newlocale/__newlocale, raise/gsignal,
ldiv/imaxdiv, strfromf128/strfromf64x). SetELFData then keeps the FIRST entry of
each address run, because disasm_funcs.emplace() does not overwrite.

std::sort is not stable, so which alias survived was decided by introsort's
partitioning of the whole vector, and that depends on the vector's length. Two
programs fused from one rootfs contain the same libraries but different numbers
of their own functions, so the same library function was named strfromf128 in
one and strfromf64x in the other.

Measured on Debian /bin/echo and /bin/cat fused with a shared library layout:
both lift 4176 library functions at 4176 IDENTICAL addresses -- the sets differ
by zero -- yet 68 of them came out under different names. With patches/0046
making the lifted name address-derived, that is the difference between two
programs sharing a compiled partition and not; it reached 66 of the 80
partitions, and it was misread once as the lifter reaching different code in the
two programs, which it does not.

Ordering by (addr, sym_name, size) makes the survivor a function of the symbol
set alone. Which alias wins is arbitrary and does not matter -- the lifted name
carries the address, and the symbol name in front of it is a diagnostic aid.
That the same one wins in every program is the point.
--- a/lifter/Binary/Loader.cpp
+++ b/lifter/Binary/Loader.cpp
@@ -247,8 +247,39 @@
   for (auto &[_, symbol] : func_symbols_map) {
     func_symbols.emplace_back(symbol);
   }
-  std::sort(func_symbols.begin(), func_symbols.end(),
-            [](auto const &lhs, auto const &rhs) { return lhs.addr < rhs.addr; });
+  // A TOTAL order, not just an ordering by address.
+  //
+  // Several symbols can name one address -- glibc is full of aliases
+  // (`newlocale`/`__newlocale`, `raise`/`gsignal`, `ldiv`/`imaxdiv`), and this
+  // vector holds every one of them, because LoadStaticSymbolsBFD appends each
+  // BSF_FUNCTION symbol directly while the eh_frame recovery adds its own
+  // entries through func_symbols_map. Only the FIRST symbol of each address run
+  // survives: SetELFData builds disasm_funcs with emplace(), which does not
+  // overwrite.
+  //
+  // Comparing on addr alone leaves the order within a run to std::sort, which
+  // is not stable, so the survivor was decided by introsort's partitioning of
+  // the whole vector -- and that depends on the vector's LENGTH. Two programs
+  // fused from one rootfs share every library symbol but have different numbers
+  // of their own, so the same library function was named `strfromf128` in one
+  // program and `strfromf64x` in the other. Measured on Debian /bin/echo and
+  // /bin/cat: identical library code at 4176 identical addresses, of which 68
+  // came out under different names. Since patches/0046 makes the lifted name
+  // address-derived, that difference is the whole reason the two programs could
+  // not share a compiled partition -- it reached 66 of 80 partitions.
+  //
+  // Ordering by (addr, sym_name, size) makes the survivor a function of the
+  // symbol set alone. Which alias wins does not matter; that it is the same one
+  // in every program does.
+  std::sort(func_symbols.begin(), func_symbols.end(), [](auto const &lhs, auto const &rhs) {
+    if (lhs.addr != rhs.addr) {
+      return lhs.addr < rhs.addr;
+    }
+    if (lhs.sym_name != rhs.sym_name) {
+      return lhs.sym_name < rhs.sym_name;
+    }
+    return lhs.size < rhs.size;
+  });
 
   /* get every dynamic symbol table */
   // LoadDynamicSymbolsBFD(); /* FIXME */
